    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build is green. The audit log confirms the change working end-to-end: an owner registered on 2026-09-04 now gets `membershipNumber=NSW-16E6609E-M27` (fiscal year FY27, since the date is after 1 July), and the new `fiscalYear` field is returned.\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved all date-derived owner values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starting 1 July).\n\n**New util** \u2014 `util/FiscalYear.java`: pure functions, mirroring `BusinessDayAdjuster`. The fiscal year starts 1 July and is named for the calendar year it ends in (Australian convention, matching the app's AU domain), so a date on/after 1 July belongs to `year + 1`.\n- `yearOf(date)` \u2192 the fiscal year as a full year\n- `label(date)` \u2192 `FY<YY>` (zero-padded last two digits)\n- `elapsed(from, to)` \u2192 fiscal-year boundaries crossed\n\n**`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`**:\n- Added a `fiscalYear` mapping \u2192 `FiscalYear.label(registrationDate)`.\n- `membershipNumber` year segment now uses `FiscalYear.yearOf(...) % 100` instead of the calendar year \u2014 a behaviour change (e.g. `-M27` instead of `-M26` for a September 2026 registration).\n- Tenure in `membershipPoints` now awards its 3 points when **at least one fiscal year has elapsed** (`FiscalYear.elapsed(registrationDate, today) >= 1`), replacing the old 365-day window.\n\nThe registration date stored by the controller is already business-day adjusted (`effectiveRegistrationDate` \u2192 `BusinessDayAdjuster.toBusinessDay`), so deriving from `owner.getRegistrationDate()` satisfies \"business-day-adjusted registration date\".\n\n**`resources/openapi.yml`**: added the `fiscalYear` property to the `Owner` schema (this generates the `OwnerDto` field the test reads) and updated the `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions to match.\n\nTest suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`. The new `coreReturnsFiscalYear` acceptance test passes (`fiscalYear` matches `FY\\d{2}`).",
